window.docContents[187].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Equality":{"contents":"\n\nnamespace EqualityPlayground\n\n\nEven Lean's equality relation is not built in.  We can define\nit ourselves:inductive Eq {α : Type} : α → α → Prop where\n  | refl : ∀ (x : α), Eq x x\n\nscoped infix:50 \" =' \" => Eq\nThe way to think about this definition (which mirrors the standard library's)\nis that, given a set α, it defines a family of propositions \"x is equal to y,\"\nparameterized by x and indexed by y from α.  There is just one way\nof constructing evidence for members of this family: applying the\nconstructor Eq.refl to a type α and a single value x : α,\nwhich yields evidence that x is equal to x.Other types of the form Eq x y where x and y are not the\nsame are thus uninhabited.We can use Eq.refl to construct evidence that, for example, 2 = 2.  Can we also use it to construct evidence that 1 + 1 = 2?\nYes, we can.  Indeed, it is the very same piece of evidence!The reason is that Lean treats as \"the same\" any two terms that are\nconvertible according to a simple set of computation rules.These rules, which are similar to those used by #eval, include\nevaluation of function application, inlining of definitions, and\nsimplification of matches.Lean terms are \"the same\" if they are convertible\naccording to a simple set of computation rules: evaluation of\nfunction applications, inlining of definitions, and simplification\nof matches.theorem four : 2 + 2 =' 1 + 3 := by\n  apply Eq.refl\nThe rfl tactic that we have used to prove\nequalities of the form x = y up to now is\nessentially just shorthand for apply Eq.refl.In tactic-based proofs of equality, the conversion rules are\nnormally hidden in uses of simp (either explicit or implicit in\nother tactics such as rfl).But you can see them directly at work in the following explicit\nproof objects:theorem four' : 2 + 2 =' 1 + 3 :=\n  Eq.refl 4\n\ntheorem singleton : ∀ (α : Type) (x : α),\n    [] ++ [x] =' x :: [] :=\n  fun (α : Type) (x : α) => Eq.refl [x]\nWe can also pattern-match on an equality proof:theorem eq_add : ∀ (n1 n2 : Nat),\n    n1 =' n2 → (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) :=\n  fun n1 n2 heq =>\n    match heq with\n    | Eq.refl _ => Eq.refl (n1 + 1)\nBy pattern-matching against n1 =' n2, we replace n2 with n1\nin the type we have to produce, so\ninstead of (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1), we now have to produce something\nof type (n1 + 1) =' (n1 + 1), which we establish by Eq.refl (n1 + 1).A tactic-based proof runs into some difficulties if we try to use\nour usual repertoire of tactics, such as rw and\nrfl. Those work with relations that Lean knows about,\nsuch as =, but not with our ='. We could teach Lean about ='\n(by registering a reflexivity lemma for rfl, or by working\nin a quotient type where =' becomes =, for rw),\nbut a simpler way is to use cases and apply instead.example (n1 n2 : Nat) (heq : n1 =' n2) :\n    (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) := by\n  rw [heq] -- doesn't work for _our_ =' relation\nexample (n1 : Nat) : (n1 + 1) =' (n1 + 1) := by\n  rfl -- doesn't work for _our_ =' relation\ntheorem eq_add' (n1 n2 : Nat) (heq : n1 =' n2) :\n    (n1 + 1) =' (n2 + 1) := by\n  cases heq -- n2 replaced by n1 in the goal!\n  apply Eq.refl\nConstruct the proof object for the following theorem. Use pattern\nmatching on the equality hypotheses.theorem eq_cons : ∀ (α : Type) (h1 h2 : α) (t1 t2 : List α),\n    h1 =' h2 → t1 =' t2 → h1 :: t1 =' h2 :: t2 :=\n  sorry\nThe inductive definition of equality implies Leibniz equality:\nwhat we mean when we say \"x and y are equal\" is that every\nproperty on P that is true of x is also true of y. Prove\nthat.theorem equality__leibniz_equality : ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    x =' y → ∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y := by\n  sorry\nConstruct the proof object for the previous exercise.  All it\nrequires is anonymous functions and pattern-matching; the large\nproof term constructed by tactics in the previous exercise is\nneedessly complicated. Hint: pattern-match as soon as possible.theorem equality__leibniz_equality_term :\n    ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    x =' y → ∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y :=\n  sorry\nShow that, in fact, the inductive definition of equality is\nequivalent to Leibniz equality.  Hint: the proof is quite short.\nYou can write it by inventing a clever property P to\ninstantiate the antecedent, or you can let Lean figure it out...theorem leibniz_equality__equality : ∀ (α : Type) (x y : α),\n    (∀ (P : α → Prop), P x → P y) → x =' y := by\n  sorry\n\n\nWhich of the following is a correct proof object for the proposition∃ x, x + 3 =' 4\n?(A) Eq.refl 4(B) @Exists.intro Nat (z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)(C) @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)(D) @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 1)(E) none of the abovetheorem quiz1 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  Eq.refl 4\ntheorem quiz2 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)\ntheorem quiz3 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 4)\ntheorem quiz4 : ∃ x, x + 3 =' 4 :=\n  @Exists.intro Nat (fun z => z + 3 =' 4) 1 (Eq.refl 1)\n\n\nend EqualityPlayground\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.7. Equality","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Equality"},"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Rewriting-with-Conditional-Statements":{"contents":"Rewriting with a conditional theorem is similar in spirit to backward\nreasoning with apply, introduced earlier in this chapter: both let\na theorem's premises become new subgoals. Here, though, it is rw\ndoing the work, using the theorem's conclusion to transform the goal rather\nthan closing it outright.\n\nSuppose that we know two numbers have the same double, and we want to\nuse this fact to rewrite one of them into the other. Recall the theorem\ndouble_injective from the previous section:#check double_injective\ndouble_injective (n m : Nat) (h : n.double = m.double) : n = m\nFor example, we can prove:\n\nexample (n m p q : Nat)\n    (h : n.double = m.double)\n    (hm : m + p = q) :\n    n + p = q := by\n  rw [double_injective n m]\n  · assumption\n  · assumption\n\n\nThe use of rw here is a little different from the examples we have seen so far.\nThe theorem double_injective says n = m\nprovided that n.double = m.double, not just n = m.\nWhen we write rw [double_injective n m], Lean uses the conclusion n = m to rewrite\nthe goal, and then asks us to prove the hypothesis needed by double_injective.\nThus we get two goals: the updated main goal, m + p = q, and the\ncondition from double_injective, n.double = m.double.\nThese goals follow by assumption from hm and h, respectively.\n\nIf we rewrite with a conditional statement of the form\nP → a = b, then Lean tries to rewrite with a = b, and then\nasks us to prove P in a new subgoal.  If the statement has more\nthan one assumption, then we get one subgoal for each assumption.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics","header":"7.6. Rewriting with Conditional Statements","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Rewriting-with-Conditional-Statements"}});